  "title": "CHAPTER 16 — INDIAN NATIONAL MOVEMENT (1857–1947)",
  "order": 683,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "4jowzvb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. BACKGROUND OF NATIONALISM"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Indian National Movement spans crucial phases from the Revolt of 1857 to national independence in 1947.",
        "The formation of the Indian National Congress (INC) in 1885 served as a primary platform for organized national movements.",
        "Early political catalysts included the Vernacular Press Act (1878) and the Ilbert Bill Controversy (1883).",
        "Important pre-independence political turning points included the Swaraj Party (1923), Communal Award (1932), Forward Bloc (1939), and Individual Satyagraha (1940).",
        "Key modern constitutional developments leading to 1947 included the August Offer (1940), Lahore Resolution (1940), Shimla Conference (1945), and Direct Action Day (16 August 1946)."
      ],
      "id": "37rvnvb"
    },
